{"qid":"Q769782","names":["Kingdom of Powys"],"title":"Kingdom of Powys","endonym":{"native":"Powys","lang":"cy"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Powys was a Welsh polity occupying the fertile Severn and Tern valleys and adjacent uplands in east-central Wales and the Welsh Marches, emerging in the fifth century from the post-Roman territory of the Cornovii under the Gwertherion dynasty. The kingdom endured sustained Mercian encroachment through the seventh and eighth centuries, losing territory progressively eastward; Cyngen ap Cadell (d. 854), last of the Gwertherion, bequeathed his kingdom to Gwynedd before dying on pilgrimage in Rome. After a period of union with Gwynedd and later reconstitution, the kingdom reached its final florescence under Madog ap Maredudd (d. 1160), whose death without a clear successor caused Powys to split irrevocably into Powys Fadog in the north and Powys Wenwynwyn in the south.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Powys's territorial extent is imperfectly known; boundaries are inferred from genealogical poems, charter witness lists, and the alignments of Offa's Dyke and Wat's Dyke rather than systematic administrative records, making cartographic precision elusive.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Powys","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Powys"},{"label":"Britannica: Powys historical kingdom","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Powys-historical-kingdom-Wales"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Madog ap Maredudd","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madog_ap_Maredudd"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Powys","rulers":[{"qid":null